[SYSDESIGN]
--- SECTION: Requirements
[SAY]Alright, so for the Facebook News Feed let me start with the functional requirements.

First, users need to be able to create posts. Second, they need to follow other users. And third, they need to view a feed of posts from people they follow, in reverse chronological order.

For non-functional requirements — we should prioritize availability over consistency here. It's totally fine if a post takes up to a minute to appear in followers' feeds. That's eventual consistency and it's acceptable for a social feed. Posting and viewing should be fast, under 500ms. We need to handle 2 billion users. And users should be able to follow an unlimited number of users and have an unlimited number of followers — this is important because it drives some key architectural decisions around fan-out.

Below the line for now: likes, comments, rich media uploads, and content ranking algorithms.[/SAY]
[DETAIL]**Functional Requirements (Core):**
1. Users can create posts
2. Users can follow other users
3. Users can view a feed of posts from followed users in reverse chronological order

**Non-Functional Requirements:**
- High availability over consistency: tolerate up to 1 minute of post staleness (eventual consistency)
- Low latency: posting and viewing feed in < 500ms
- Massive scale: 2B total users
- Unlimited follows: users can follow and be followed by unlimited users

**Below the Line (Out of Scope):**
- Likes, comments, reactions
- Rich media / image uploads
- Content ranking algorithms
- Notifications[/DETAIL]
--- SECTION: Core Entities
[SAY]Three core entities here.

A User is anyone in the system. A Follow is a uni-directional link — I follow you, but you don't necessarily follow me back. And a Post is content created by a user that shows up in the feeds of their followers.[/SAY]
[DETAIL]- **User** : A user in our system.
- **Follow** : A uni-directional link between users in our system.
- **Post** : A post made by a user in our system. Posts can be made by any user, and are shown in the feed of users who follow the poster.[/DETAIL]
--- SECTION: API Design
[SAY]Okay so three endpoints, one per functional requirement.

First, POST /posts with a content body. I'm keeping the content structure flexible to support rich content later. Returns the new postId.

Second, PUT /users/:id/follow to follow a user. I'm using PUT here because it's idempotent — if someone accidentally taps follow twice, the second request is a no-op. The authenticated user from the JWT is the follower, and the :id in the path is who they're following. Unfollowing would be a DELETE to the same path, but that's out of scope.

Third, GET /feed with pageSize and cursor params for pagination. The cursor is a timestamp representing the oldest post the user has seen. Each page returns N posts older than that timestamp. This gives us clean infinite scroll behavior.

I'm deliberately not spending time on post structure or media attachments — those are straightforward and I'd rather save time for the interesting fan-out challenges.[/SAY]
[DETAIL]The API is the primary interface that users will interact with. It's important to define the API early on, as it will guide your high-level design. We can build our API by defining the endpoints necessary for each of our functional requirements.
For our first requirement, we need to create posts:
We'll leave the content empty to account for rich content or other structured data we might want to include in the post. For authentication, we'll tell our interviewer that authentication tokens are included in the header of the request and avoid going into too much detail there unless requested.
Moving on, we need to be able to follow people. Let's use a simple RESTFUL PUT endpoint for this. The authenticated user (from their JWT) is the one doing the following, and `[id]` is the user they want to follow.
Here the follow action is binary. By using PUT we can ensure it is idempotent so it doesn't fail if the user clicks follow twice. Unfollowing (out of scope) would be accomplished with a DELETE. We don't need a body but we'll include a stub just in case and keep moving.
Our last requirement is to be able to view our feed and page through it.
This can be a simple GET request. We've included some parameters to allow the user to page through their feed. Since our requirements are to return posts in reverse chronological order, when users page through their feed they'll be looking at increasingly older posts. We'll use a timestamp as a "cursor" to represent the oldest post they've seen so far, so each page will return N posts older than that timestamp.
We'll avoid diving into the structure of Posts for the moment to give ourselves time for the juicier parts of the interview.
Especially for more senior candidates, it's important to focus your efforts on the "interesting" aspects of the interview. Spending too much time on obvious elements both deprives you of time for the more interesting parts of the interview but also signals to the interviewer that you may not be able to distinguish more complex pieces from trivial ones: a critical skill for senior engineers. "I'll come back if I have time for this" is a great strategy!
Ok, we now have some API's, let's work on building the high-level design behind them.

